## 1. Exact correlated slack decomposition

Put

\[
x=U-|S|,\qquad y=|S|-2\alpha,\qquad
z=2\alpha-C,\qquad w=C-M.
\]

Canonical Section 101 gives the chain

\[
M\le C\le2\alpha\le |S|\le U,
\]

so every currency is nonnegative and

\[
\boxed{x+y+z+w=U-M=\sigma.}
\]

Section 118 is therefore entitled to keep the four currencies correlated rather than
spending a fresh copy of the full `sigma` at every later estimate.

## 2. Gap and tail geometry spend only `x` and `w`

Write the largest complement gap as `g_1=theta-delta`, and put
`phi=2pi-2theta`. The canonical transfer function gives

\[
h_\theta(\delta)\le w.
\]

On the whole Section-118 window its derivative on `0<=delta<=0.1` stays above `0.30`,
while the alternative `delta>=0.1` costs more than `0.0326`. Since
`w<=sigma<=0.0088`, that alternative is impossible, hence

\[
\boxed{\delta\le{10\over3}w.}
\]

Writing `g_2=phi+x_2`, `g_3=phi+x_3` and
`tau=sum_{j>=4} g_j`, the exact complement-measure identity is

\[
x_2+x_3+\tau=\delta+x.
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\tau\le x+{10\over3}w,
\qquad
C_{tail}\le{x\over2}+{5\over3}w.
}
\]

The adjacent-support-span argument also spends only `w`, contributing endpoint
capacity `20w/3`.

At the endpoint, `(10/3)sigma=0.029333...`, still far below `0.1`, and the oriented
main tangent region remains shorter than `pi`. Thus the scale-free Section-104
finite-cover/tangent geometry is still inside its actual hypotheses.

## 3. The face currency must be taken on one common subsequence

Pass to one further subsequence on which

\[
Q_D/D\to q,\qquad c_D/D\to c,\qquad s_D/D\to s_0.
\]

The retained finite Section-101 face currency gives

\[
\boxed{2s_0+c+q\le z},
\]

and hence

\[
\boxed{q+c\le z.}
\]

This is a genuine joint inequality. Separate limsups of `q` and `c` could not be
added this way; the Section-118 proof correctly uses one common subsequence.

## 4. Hostile check of the refined exceptional-micro-route bound

The delicate new bookkeeping claim is

\[
\boxed{\text{exceptional micro endpoint density}\le3z+6c.}
\]

Canonical Section 104 partitions the exceptional micro routes into three disjoint
classes.

1. A direct one-edge loaded-loaded micro side is one loaded-loaded contact, so its
   normalized occurrence density is at most `c+o(1)`.
2. Non-direct micro routes have total normalized excess at most `z+c+o(1)`. For a
   route of length `k>=3`, the baseline excess is `k-2` and
   `k<=3(k-2)`, so their total edge-occurrence density is at most
   `3(z+c)+o(1)`.
3. A length-two micro route with loaded middle vertex uses two loaded-loaded contact
   occurrences, giving total density at most `2c+o(1)`.

Adding the three classes yields exactly

\[
c+3(z+c)+2c=3z+6c.
\]

There is no missing factor of two from physical edge occurrences.

The other non-tooth capacities are

\[
5q
\]

for bounded nonquadrilateral boundary and

\[
z+C_{tail}
\]

for nonprincipal macroscopic routes. Thus the complete non-tooth total is

\[
4z+5q+6c+C_{tail}.
\]

Since `q+c<=z`,

\[
5q+6c\le6(q+c)\le6z,
\]

and therefore

\[
\boxed{
4z+5q+6c+C_{tail}\le10z+C_{tail}.
}
\]

This is the sharp companion's claimed shared currency.

## 5. Teeth spend only `y`

Canonical Section 104 gives the exact base-excess budget

\[
\limsup_D{1\over D}
\sum_{\rm micro\ bases}(|p_k-p_{k+1}|-1)
\le{|S|\over2}-\alpha={y\over2}.
\]

For one projective endpoint-direction interval of width `epsilon`, the hostile-audited
tangent-window theorem gives, for either leg choice,

\[
\limsup_D{N([0,X])\over D}
\le C_{tail}+{X+\epsilon\over2}.
\]

The same quantile/bathtub argument as in Section 117 yields

\[
q_1+q_2
\le Q_{tooth}(y)+2C_{tail}+\epsilon,
\]

where `Q_tooth(y)` is the unique nonnegative solution of

\[
\sin Q+\sqrt3(1-\cos Q)-Q={y\over2}.
\]

If both tooth legs lie in the same width-`epsilon` projective interval,
triangle-freeness forces the near-`pi` apex branch. The base excess is at least
`2cos(epsilon/2)-1`, so after `epsilon->0` the double-leg endpoint density costs at
most `y`.

Hence the full regular-tooth capacity is

\[
\boxed{Q_{tooth}(y)+y+2C_{tail}.}
\]

Combining this with the non-tooth estimate gives

\[
\boxed{
B(x,y,z,w)
\le Q_{tooth}(y)+y+{3\over2}x+10z+{35\over3}w.
}
\]

## 6. The slack-simplex maximization is analytic

Because

\[
{3\over2}<10<{35\over3}
\]

and `x+y+z+w=sigma`,

\[
B(x,y,z,w)
\le {35\over3}\sigma
+Q_{tooth}(y)-{32\over3}y.
\]

Let

\[
F(Q)=\sin Q+\sqrt3(1-\cos Q)-Q.
\]

Then `F(Q_tooth(y))=y/2`, while

\[
F'(Q)=\cos Q+\sqrt3\sin Q-1,
\]

and

\[
F''(Q)=-\sin Q+\sqrt3\cos Q>0
\qquad(0<Q<\pi/3).
\]

Thus `F'` is strictly increasing and the correction

\[
Q_{tooth}(y)-{32\over3}y
\]

has one global maximum, at

\[
F'(Q_0)={3\over64}.
\]

Independent high-precision recomputation gives

\[
Q_0=0.0272815201203264009\ldots,
\]

\[
y_0=0.0012822850180674681\ldots,
\]

\[
\kappa_0
=Q_0-{32\over3}y_0
=0.0136038132609400741\ldots .
\]

Therefore the global bad-direction capacity satisfies

\[
\boxed{
B\le B_*(\sigma):={35\over3}\sigma+\kappa_0.
}
\]

The use of the unrestricted maximum is safe even when `sigma<y_0`: restricting
`0<=y<=sigma` can only lower the correction.

## 7. Section 117 signed-return transport applies unchanged

The hostile-audited Section-117 finite theorem is

\[
dE_D\ge(2-\sqrt3)A_{gg,D}
\]

with

\[
A_{gg,D}
\ge A_{4,D}-2T_DM_{bad}(\epsilon)-{\Delta_D\over\epsilon}.
\]

Section 118 changes only the upper bound on `M_bad(epsilon)/D`. It does not alter train
orientation, physical endpoint injectivity, the three-host constant, exact train-area
transport, or the order of limits. Reuse of Section 117 is therefore legitimate.

The Section-107 circular-segment identity gives the conservative area floor

\[
A_*(\sigma)
={\pi\over4}
-G(\theta)
-2G\left(\phi_8+{10\sigma\over3}\right)
-G\left({10\sigma\over3}\right).
\]

Canonical Sections 82 and 89 plus the retained face currency give

\[
{T_D\over D}\le C_T(\sigma)+o(1),
\qquad
C_T=U(\theta)+2\sigma.
\]

The selected principal-route excess is conservatively at most `sigma D+o(D)`. Hence
every survivor in the safe window must satisfy

\[
\boxed{
\lambda\sigma
\ge
(2-\sqrt3)
\left[A_*(\sigma)-2C_T(\sigma)B_*(\sigma)\right]_+,
}
\]

with `lambda=sin(theta/2)`.

## 8. Endpoint arithmetic and monotonicity

At `sigma=0.0088`, independent high-precision computation gives

\[
\theta=2.70477923521549347\ldots,
\qquad
\lambda=0.976243913742568741\ldots,
\]

\[
A_*=0.470375286800424054\ldots,
\]

\[
C_T=1.84875239846689394\ldots,
\qquad
B_*=0.116270479927606741\ldots .
\]

Thus

\[
A_*-2C_TB_*
=0.040464629526304432\ldots>0.
\]

The signed-return lower bound forces normalized route excess

\[
{2-\sqrt3\over\lambda}
\left(A_*-2C_TB_*\right)
=0.011106307195331664\ldots,
\]

while only `0.0088` is available. The contradiction margin is therefore

\[
\boxed{0.002306307195331664\ldots>0.}
\]

On this neighborhood, increasing slack increases `theta`, `lambda`, `C_T`, and `B_*`,
and decreases `A_*`; the available budget also increases. Hence the contradiction
margin is decreasing. Positivity at `0.0088` closes every smaller positive slack.

The supplied verifier
`verify_target_c_correlated_slack_sharp_118.py` reproduces these constants and gives
the relaxed scalar zero

\[
\sigma_{env}=0.008975876224476734\ldots,
\qquad
\mu_{env}=0.976247964248172612\ldots .
\]

That equality root is not needed for, and is not asserted as, the safe theorem endpoint.

## 9. Disposition

The corrected sharp Section-118 correlated-slack theorem survives hostile review.
The valid proof-only theorem is

\[
\boxed{
\sigma\le0.0088
\Longrightarrow
\operatorname{diam}(P_*)
\ge(0.9762439137425688\ldots-o(1))D.
}
\]

This strictly supersedes the Section-117 numerical endpoint while retaining its
signed-return geometry as a dependency.

It remains a quantitative carrier theorem. It does not prove that every Type-II
survivor lies in the small-slack regime, does not force an exact unit equilateral
triangle, does not construct a same-cardinality strict sub-`D` source, and does not
address the macroscopic inactive-container branch.

**Review verdict:** PASS as proof-only. **Theorem state unchanged.**
